qb('design', [
{n:1, q:`Do you like Gmail's Smart Compose?`,
tests:`Whether you can form a real opinion, back it with criteria, and turn critique into improvements and metrics.`,
answer:`<p>Yes, I do, and I think it's one of the better examples of AI done quietly. Let me say how I'm judging it, then where it falls short.</p>
<p><strong>The job it does.</strong> Most email isn't creative writing. It's "Thanks, see you Tuesday" and "Attached is the deck." Smart Compose takes the most repetitive part of writing and removes a few keystrokes at a time without asking you to change how you work.</p>
<p><strong>Why the design works.</strong> What I admire most is the cost of being wrong. The suggestion is grey text that you accept with Tab or ignore by typing. A bad suggestion costs you nothing, so the model can be wrong fairly often and the feature still feels helpful. That's the right shape for a prediction product: cheap to accept, free to ignore. It also appears exactly where your attention already is, so there's nothing to learn.</p>
<p><strong>Where it falls short.</strong> Three things. First, sameness: if everyone accepts the same phrasing, email starts to sound generic, and some people notice that in messages they receive. Second, subtle errors: when you're moving fast you can accept a completion that says something slightly different from what you meant, like "I'll send it tomorrow" when you meant next week. Third, trust: a lot of people quietly wonder what the model is learning from their writing, and the product doesn't really answer that.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd change.</strong> I'd let people pick a tone, like more formal or more casual, because it addresses sameness and makes the feature feel personal. And I'd add a one-line "why am I seeing this, and what's used to make it" explanation in settings, because trust problems are cheap to address early and expensive later.</p>
<p><strong>How I'd know it's working.</strong> Acceptance rate is the obvious metric, but on its own it can mislead, because people accept then fix. So I'd pair it with the edit rate after accepting, and characters saved per email. If acceptance goes up and post-accept edits go down, the suggestions are genuinely getting better.</p>
<p>So overall: a clear yes for routine email, with tone controls and a transparency line as the next two things I'd ship.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Give a verdict in the first sentence.</li><li>Say what job the product does.</li><li>Explain why the design works, with one specific insight (here, the cost of being wrong).</li><li>Name two or three weaknesses.</li><li>Suggest improvements tied to those weaknesses.</li><li>Give metrics, including one that catches misleading usage.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>The "cost of being wrong" point is a real product insight, not a description of features.</li><li>The metrics pair a usage number with a quality check.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Hedging ("it has pros and cons"). Commit to an opinion.</li><li>Listing features instead of explaining why they work.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What would you do if acceptance rate is high but user satisfaction is flat?`,`I'd check whether people are accepting then editing, which means the suggestions feel close but not right. I'd segment by email type, because suggestions might be great for scheduling notes and poor for anything personal. Then I'd consider showing fewer, higher-confidence suggestions.`],[`How would you extend Smart Compose to a new surface?`,`I'd pick a surface with the same shape: lots of repetitive text and a low cost of being wrong. Customer support replies in Google Chat or form fields in Docs fit. I'd avoid anything where a wrong completion has real consequences, like financial or legal text.`]]},

{n:2, q:`Tell me about a product you think is poorly designed.`,
tests:`Whether you can diagnose a design problem precisely, from the user's point of view, and propose fixes that follow from the diagnosis.`,
answer:`<p>I'll pick airport self check-in kiosks, because they fail in a very specific and fixable way.</p>
<p><strong>Who uses it and what they want.</strong> The main user is a traveler with a checked bag who wants to get from the door to bag drop in about two minutes. They're often stressed, holding a phone and a passport, and maybe managing kids. The kiosk's whole job is to be faster than the counter.</p>
<p><strong>Where it breaks.</strong> Three places.</p>
<p>First, the start screen. It offers five or six options with equal visual weight: scan passport, enter confirmation code, frequent flyer number, and so on. At the exact moment the user wants a single obvious next step, they have to make a decision. You can watch people hesitate.</p>
<p>Second, upsells in the middle of the task. Before your bag tag prints, you're asked about seat upgrades, extra legroom, and lounge access. Commercially I understand why, but it interrupts the one thing the user came to do, and many people mis-tap because they're rushing.</p>
<p>Third, errors. When something fails, like a passport that won't scan or a booking that needs agent review, the screen says what went wrong but not what to do next. So people stand there, and then join the staffed queue anyway.</p>
<p><strong>The real cost.</strong> The kiosk was supposed to take load off staff, but staff now spend their time rescuing people from the machine instead of helping the travelers who genuinely need a person.</p>
<p><strong>How I'd fix it.</strong> One primary action per screen, with scanning the passport as the default because it works for almost everyone. Move all upsells to after the bag tag prints, when the user is relaxed and has a moment. Rewrite every error to name the next step, like "Please go to counter 4, no need to queue." And add a help button that's always visible and actually calls someone over.</p>
<p><strong>How I'd measure it.</strong> The share of travelers who finish without staff help, median time from start to bag tag, and, to make sure the business doesn't lose out, upsell conversion after moving it to the end. My bet is that last number holds up, because people buy more when they're not stressed.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Name one product and say why you picked it.</li><li>Define the user and their goal.</li><li>Walk through two or three specific failures.</li><li>Name the real consequence.</li><li>Propose fixes that map one-to-one to the failures.</li><li>Give metrics, including the business trade-off.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Concrete, observable details ("you can watch people hesitate").</li><li>Acknowledging the business reason for the upsell, then solving for both sides.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Picking a product the interviewer's company makes, unless you're very confident.</li><li>Vague complaints like "the UI is cluttered."</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`The airline says upsells at that step drive a lot of revenue. How do you respond?`,`I'd propose an A/B test: upsell at the end versus in the middle, measuring revenue per passenger and completion time. If revenue drops, I'd try a compromise, like a single, dismissible upsell after the seat is confirmed. But I'd want data, because stressed users often skip offers entirely.`],[`How would you prioritize among your fixes?`,`The error messages first. They're cheap to change and they drive the most staff intervention. Then the start screen, then moving upsells, which needs commercial sign-off.`]]},

{n:3, q:`How would you improve Google Maps for people who walk in cities?`,
tests:`Whether you can choose a focused user, find a real unmet need, and prioritize one solution with clear reasoning.`,
answer:`<p>Before I dive in, can I check two things? Is the goal more walking trips on Maps, or better experiences for people already walking? And is there a particular market in mind?</p>
<p><em>(If the interviewer says it's up to you:)</em> Then I'll aim for people trusting Maps enough to use it for every walk, and I'll think about large cities globally. I'll go through who I'm building for, what's hard for them today, a few ideas, and then pick one.</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> There are tourists, commuters, people walking for exercise, and people with mobility needs. I'll focus on daily urban commuters, meaning people who walk fifteen to thirty minutes a day to transit or work. They walk the most, so they give us the most signal, and they're the group most likely to stop opening Maps once they think they know the route.</p>
<p><strong>What's hard for them.</strong> Three things stand out. First, the fastest route isn't always the best one on foot: it might be unlit, have no shade in summer, or run along a highway. Second, the last hundred meters is hard: which exit from the station, which entrance to a big building. Third, the blue dot is unreliable between tall buildings, so the first turn is often wrong.</p>
<p>The one I'd prioritize is the first, because it's about whether the route feels right, and that's what decides whether someone trusts Maps for walking at all. Exit and GPS problems are real, but they're more occasional.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: route options that weigh comfort, like "shadier," "better lit," or "quieter," alongside "fastest." Two: station exit guidance, telling you which exit to take before you reach the gates. Three: a quick compass calibration and street-level arrows for the first turn.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> I'd start with comfort-aware routing, launched first as a "well-lit route" option in the evening. Safety at night is the strongest reason people pick a route that isn't the fastest, and Google already has signals like Street View imagery, business density, and aggregated foot traffic to estimate it. I'd launch in a few cities where that data is strongest, and be careful with naming: we'd describe it as "busier, well-lit streets," not claim a route is safe.</p>
<p><strong>How I'd know it's working.</strong> The main metric would be weekly walking navigation users who come back the next week. I'd also watch how often people choose the comfort option, and how often they abandon or reroute. The guardrail is added time: if the recommended route is usually more than a few minutes longer, people will ignore it.</p>
<p>So in short: focus on commuters, help them choose how a walk feels, start with well-lit routes at night, and measure repeat use.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Clarify the goal and scope, then state an assumption.</li><li>List user segments and choose one, with a reason.</li><li>Name two or three pain points and prioritize one.</li><li>Brainstorm three solutions, then pick one.</li><li>Explain why it's feasible (existing data) and the risks.</li><li>Give metrics and a guardrail, then summarize.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>The segment choice has a reason tied to the product goal.</li><li>The recommendation is narrowed to a launchable first version.</li><li>It flags a real risk (claiming "safe" routes).</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Brainstorming ten features without choosing.</li><li>Skipping why this user matters to the business.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you estimate how well-lit a street is?`,`Combine signals: Street View imagery at night where it exists, municipal streetlight data, business opening hours, and aggregated, anonymized foot traffic after dark. I'd validate against a sample of streets with human ratings before launching.`],[`What if walking trips go up but people complain routes are longer?`,`I'd cap the added time, for example no more than three minutes or ten percent longer, and show the difference clearly so people make an informed choice.`],[`Why not focus on tourists?`,`Tourists have bigger needs per trip but walk in one city for a few days. Commuters walk every day, so improvements compound into habit, which matters more for Maps long term.`]]},

{n:4, q:`Design an alarm clock for people who are deaf or hard of hearing.`,
tests:`Empathy for a specific user, creativity beyond the obvious, and practical trade-offs like reliability and cost.`,
answer:`<p>Let me clarify a couple of things first. Should I design a physical device, an app, or either? And is this only for waking up, or for alerts in general?</p>
<p><em>(Assuming it's open:)</em> I'll treat it as waking up reliably, and I'm open to hardware because phones on their own can't do much here.</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> The group ranges from people who are profoundly deaf to people with mild hearing loss who take hearing aids out at night. I'll focus on adults who live independently and have to get up at a set time for work, because for them a missed alarm has real consequences, and many of them are currently using workarounds.</p>
<p><strong>What's hard today.</strong> Existing options are a vibrating pad under the pillow or a flashing lamp. The problems I'd expect are: the pad slides away during the night so it doesn't work; a flashing light doesn't wake people who sleep facing away or are very tired; and many people share a bed, so a strong vibration or light wakes their partner too. Underneath all that, the most important need is trust: people set two or three alarms because they can't be sure one will work.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: a wearable, like a wristband, that vibrates in an escalating pattern. It stays on you, so it can't slide away, and it only wakes the wearer. Two: a smart light that simulates sunrise over thirty minutes, then flashes, for a gentle wake plus a backup. Three: a bed-shaker with an app that confirms it's plugged in and in place before you sleep.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> The wristband, as the core product, with an optional bedside light that works with it. The wristband solves the biggest need, reliability, and it's also the only option that doesn't disturb a partner. The key design choices are: escalating vibration that doesn't stop until you press and hold a button, so you can't dismiss it in your sleep; a check before bed that confirms battery and that the alarm is set, shown with a big clear icon; and a backup: if the band isn't dismissed within five minutes, the bedside light or a paired phone flashes and can message a chosen contact.</p>
<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Battery life is the big one. A dead band is a missed alarm, so I'd make charging a daytime routine and warn clearly when it's low. Comfort matters too, since people won't wear something uncomfortable all night.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> The north star is the share of alarms dismissed within five minutes of going off, meaning the person actually woke up. I'd also track nights worn per week and, in interviews, whether people stopped setting backup alarms, which is the clearest sign of trust.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Clarify form factor and scope.</li><li>Choose a segment with real stakes.</li><li>Identify pain points and find the underlying need (trust).</li><li>Three ideas, one pick, with specific design decisions.</li><li>Risks, then metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Finding the need under the needs: people set multiple alarms because they don't trust one.</li><li>Thinking about the bed partner, which most candidates miss.</li><li>Design details that solve failure modes (hold-to-dismiss, backup escalation).</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Designing only for profoundly deaf users without acknowledging the range.</li><li>Assuming a phone app alone can solve it.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you validate this before building hardware?`,`Interview ten to fifteen people from the community about their current setup and failures, then test a prototype using an existing fitness band with a custom vibration pattern for two weeks, tracking whether they woke on time.`],[`How would you price it?`,`Close to a mid-range fitness band, and I'd explore whether it qualifies for disability support programs or insurance reimbursement, which can matter more than the sticker price.`]]},

{n:5, q:`Design a mobile app for roommates to split shared expenses.`,
tests:`Whether you can find a sharper angle in a crowded category and design for social friction, not just math.`,
answer:`<p>This space already has apps like Splitwise, so before I design, I'd want to ask: are we a new startup, or adding this to something like Google Pay? That changes whether we can move money directly.</p>
<p><em>(Assuming it's a feature inside a payments app:)</em> Great, then we can settle up inside the product, which is a real advantage.</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> Roommates in their twenties sharing an apartment for a year or more. They split rent, utilities, groceries, and random items like toilet paper. This is different from splitting a dinner once: it's a long relationship where money awkwardness builds up.</p>
<p><strong>What's actually hard.</strong> The math is easy. The hard parts are social. One: someone has to remember to log every expense, and it's usually the same person, who starts to resent it. Two: asking a friend to pay you back feels awkward, so balances grow and become a source of tension. Three: shared recurring bills like internet are paid by one person, and everyone forgets the monthly split.</p>
<p>I'd prioritize the awkwardness of asking, because that's what damages the relationship, and the other two problems make it worse.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: automatic tracking. Link the household's recurring bills so they split themselves each month, no logging needed. Two: "neutral reminders." The app, not the roommate, sends a gentle reminder on a predictable schedule, like the first of the month, so nobody has to be the bad guy. Three: a shared household balance that settles automatically on a set date, so money moves without anyone asking.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> A household with a monthly settle-up day. Recurring bills get added once and split automatically. Other expenses are added by snapping a receipt. On settle-up day, everyone sees one number, what they owe or are owed, and pays with one tap. The key design decision is that the app initiates the request, not a person. That turns an awkward conversation into a routine.</p>
<p><strong>What could go wrong.</strong> Disputes, like "I didn't agree to that expensive olive oil." So any roommate can flag an item before settle-up day, and it's excluded until they resolve it. And not everyone will want automatic payments, so that has to be opt-in.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> The north star is households that complete settle-up two months in a row, because that shows the routine is working. Supporting metrics are the share of expenses added by someone other than the household creator, which shows the logging burden is shared, and the average time an outstanding balance stays open.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Acknowledge competitors and clarify context.</li><li>Define a specific user and relationship.</li><li>Reframe the problem: social, not mathematical.</li><li>Three ideas, one design with a key decision.</li><li>Risks and edge cases, then metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>The reframe from math to social friction is the insight interviewers remember.</li><li>A metric that captures shared burden, not just usage.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Spending the answer on UI details like buttons and tabs.</li><li>Ignoring the existing competition.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you get the second roommate to join?`,`Make the invite useful before they install: they can see and pay their share from a link. Then the app becomes the easiest way to see the balance, which pulls them in.`],[`How would this make money?`,`Inside a payments app, it drives payment volume and daily habit, which is the business value. On its own, I'd consider a small fee for instant transfers, not a subscription.`]]},

{n:6, q:`Redesign a home thermostat.`,
tests:`Whether you can question the premise of an everyday product and design around what people actually want from it.`,
answer:`<p>I'd like to start by asking what "the thermostat" is for. Nobody wants to set a temperature. They want to be comfortable, and many want to spend less on energy. So I'll redesign around those two goals rather than around the dial.</p>
<p>Is it okay if I assume a connected thermostat in a typical home, and that we're a hardware and software company?</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> Households split roughly into people who set it once and forget it, people who fiddle with it constantly, and people who care about the energy bill. I'll focus on busy families who care about the bill but don't have time to manage it. They're the biggest group, and they're where a smart thermostat most often underdelivers: people buy one expecting savings, then leave the schedule on default.</p>
<p><strong>Pain points.</strong> Schedules are tedious to set up, so people don't. Different people in the home want different temperatures, which leads to thermostat wars. And people have no idea what their changes cost, so they can't make trade-offs.</p>
<p>I'd prioritize the last one. If people could see the cost of their choices, they'd make better ones, and it makes the other problems easier to discuss at home.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: show cost in money, not degrees. Turning it up two degrees shows "about eight dollars more this month." Two: learning schedules that notice when the house is empty from phones and motion, so there's nothing to set up. Three: room-level comfort with small sensors, so the bedroom can be warm without heating the whole house.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> I'd combine the first two into one simple experience. The main screen shows how comfortable the home is and what it's costing this month against a budget the household sets. When someone changes the temperature, it shows the cost of that change in plain terms. Behind the scenes, it learns occupancy and eases off when the house is empty, and it tells you what it did: "Saved $4 this week while you were out."</p>
<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Getting the cost estimate wrong would destroy trust, so I'd show ranges at first and use the utility's real rates where we can connect. Learning mistakes, like cooling the house when someone is working from home, are frustrating, so there's always a one-tap "I'm home" override.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Monthly energy saved against a baseline for similar homes, the number of manual overrides (fewer means the learning works), and retention of the budget feature after three months.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Reframe the product's real job.</li><li>Clarify context, choose a segment with a reason.</li><li>Pain points, prioritize one.</li><li>Ideas, then combine into one coherent experience.</li><li>Risks and trust, then metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Opening with a reframe ("nobody wants to set a temperature") signals product thinking immediately.</li><li>Manual overrides as a metric is a clever inverse signal.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Redesigning the dial's appearance and stopping there.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How do you handle two people who want different temperatures?`,`Room sensors help, but socially I'd let each person set a comfort range and show the compromise and its cost. Making the trade-off visible tends to end the argument faster than any feature.`],[`How would you work with energy utilities?`,`Utilities pay for demand response, meaning slightly reducing heating or cooling at peak times. With consent, the thermostat can join those programs and pass savings to the household, which also helps the business model.`]]},

{n:7, q:`Design a product that helps someone stay connected with a parent who lives alone abroad.`,
tests:`Empathy, handling two users with different needs, and sensitivity around privacy and dignity.`,
answer:`<p>This one has two users, the adult child and the parent, and I think designing for both is the key to getting it right. Can I ask how old the parent is, roughly, and whether we're thinking about health monitoring or emotional connection?</p>
<p><em>(Assuming a parent in their late sixties or seventies, healthy but aging, and the focus is connection:)</em> Great. I'll go through both users, their needs, then pick what to build.</p>
<p><strong>The adult child.</strong> They live in another country, often with a big time difference and a busy life. Their need is two-sided: they want to feel close, and they want reassurance that the parent is okay. The worry is often the bigger emotion, especially after a missed call.</p>
<p><strong>The parent.</strong> They may be less comfortable with technology, and they want connection without feeling like a burden or being watched. Dignity matters a lot. Many older parents would rather hide a problem than worry their kids.</p>
<p><strong>The core tension</strong> is that the child wants reassurance and the parent doesn't want surveillance. A good product gives the child peace of mind through connection, not monitoring.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: a daily "I'm okay" check-in, a single tap or photo from the parent, that the child sees in their morning. Two: a shared photo frame for the parent's home that shows new photos from family and lets the parent react with one touch, which in turn tells the child they're active. Three: time-zone-aware call scheduling that suggests good windows for both people and makes calling one tap for the parent.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> The shared frame, with the one-touch reaction. For the parent, it's a gift, not a monitoring device: it brings grandkids' photos into the living room with nothing to learn. For the child, each reaction is a warm signal that the parent is up and well, without a camera or tracking. I'd add a large "call" button on the frame that rings the family, and a gentle nudge to the child if there hasn't been a reaction in a day or two. The parent controls whether that nudge is on.</p>
<p><strong>Risks.</strong> It must work on setup day without the parent configuring anything: the child sets it up remotely, and it just needs Wi-Fi. And we need to be careful the nudge doesn't create false alarms, since the parent might just be traveling, so there's an easy "I'm away" setting.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Weekly reactions from the parent, calls initiated from the frame, and a quarterly survey question to both sides: "Do you feel closer?" For the child, I'd also ask whether they feel less worried.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Name both users and clarify scope.</li><li>Needs for each user separately.</li><li>Name the tension between them.</li><li>Ideas, then pick the one that resolves the tension.</li><li>Setup and false-alarm risks, then metrics for both sides.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Naming the core tension (reassurance versus surveillance).</li><li>Giving the parent control, which shows respect for dignity.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Jumping to cameras, location tracking, or health sensors without discussing consent.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you expand this later?`,`With consent, optional health integrations, like a fall-detection watch, for families who want them. I'd keep it clearly separate and opt-in by the parent.`],[`How would you reach these users?`,`Target the adult child, who's the buyer, through gifting moments like Mother's Day, Father's Day, and holidays.`]]},

{n:8, q:`How would you improve Google Ads onboarding for a first-time small business advertiser?`,
tests:`Understanding of the ads business and small business owners, and balancing their success with Google's revenue.`,
answer:`<p>The goal I'd set for onboarding isn't just "launch a campaign." It's "get a first result the owner believes in," because a small business that doesn't see value in the first month usually churns. Does that match how you'd think about it?</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> I'll focus on a local service business, like a plumber or a dental practice, run by the owner, who has a website but no marketing staff. They have maybe a few hundred dollars a month to spend and very little time.</p>
<p><strong>What's hard for them.</strong> First, the vocabulary: keywords, match types, bidding strategies. It all feels like a test they might fail. Second, they don't know what "good" looks like, so after launch they can't tell whether it's working. Third, and most important, tracking. Many never set up conversion tracking, so the account shows clicks and spend but not calls or bookings. Without that, they can't see value, and the automated bidding can't optimize either.</p>
<p>I'd prioritize tracking, because it fixes the owner's confidence problem and the performance problem at the same time.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: make "how do customers contact you?" part of the first step, then set up tracking for that automatically: call tracking for phone numbers, or a simple form-submission tag. Two: a plain-language weekly summary: "Your ads brought 12 calls this week, about $18 per call." Three: benchmarks: "Plumbers near you typically pay $15 to $30 per call."</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> I'd rebuild onboarding around the outcome. The first question isn't about keywords, it's "What do you want customers to do: call, book, or visit?" Based on that answer, we set up tracking for that action automatically, suggest a budget with an estimate of results, and build the campaign with sensible defaults. After launch, the dashboard leads with that one outcome and cost per outcome, compared against local benchmarks.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-offs.</strong> Simpler onboarding means fewer controls, and some advertisers will want them. So "advanced settings" should always be reachable, just not in the way. There's also a revenue question: better tracking might lead some advertisers to spend less if results are poor. I think that's acceptable, because advertisers who can see results stay longer and spend more over time.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> The north star is the share of new advertisers with at least one tracked conversion in their first thirty days. Supporting that: ninety-day retention, and time to first launch. The guardrail is support contacts per new advertiser.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Redefine the goal of onboarding.</li><li>Specific user with constraints.</li><li>Pain points, prioritize one that solves two problems.</li><li>Ideas and a redesigned flow.</li><li>Business trade-offs, then metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Connecting user confidence to algorithm performance shows domain knowledge.</li><li>Addressing the revenue tension openly.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Proposing a tutorial or video as the main fix.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What if the advertiser doesn't have a website?`,`Let them advertise a phone number or a Google Business Profile, and track calls. Many local businesses live on their profile, not a site.`],[`How would you test the new onboarding?`,`An A/B test on new sign-ups, measuring thirty-day tracked conversions and ninety-day retention. Because retention takes time, I'd use tracked conversions as the early read.`]]},

{n:9, q:`Design a way for advertisers to see whether their ads truly drive sales.`,
tests:`Understanding of incrementality versus attribution, and making a technically hard concept usable.`,
answer:`<p>The key word here is "truly." Most reporting today shows attribution: which ad a customer touched before buying. But some of those customers would have bought anyway. What advertisers really want to know is incrementality: how many sales happened because of the ads. So I'll design around that.</p>
<p>Can I assume we're talking about mid-sized advertisers who spend enough to run a test, but don't have a data science team?</p>
<p><strong>Their needs.</strong> They need a trustworthy answer to "if I turned this off, how many sales would I lose?" They need it in plain language, without a statistics degree. And they need it to lead to a decision, like increasing or cutting budget.</p>
<p><strong>Why it's hard.</strong> The honest way to measure this is a controlled experiment, where some people or regions don't see the ads, and you compare. But experiments cost money, because you're deliberately not advertising to some people, and they need enough volume to produce a clear result. Many advertisers never run one because it feels complicated and risky.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: a guided experiment builder that picks a design, like holding out some regions, and estimates cost and duration upfront. Two: always-on modeled incrementality, using past experiments to estimate the incremental share for each campaign. Three: a simple comparison view showing attributed sales next to incremental sales.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> The guided experiment builder first, because it's the source of truth, and models are only credible when they're calibrated by real experiments. The flow is: pick the campaign and the question, "Is this campaign worth its budget?" The tool proposes a design, for example a geography-based test, and says "This will take about four weeks and cost roughly five percent of your spend." After the test, the result reads: "Your ads drove about 1,200 extra sales, plus or minus 200. That's $22 per incremental sale." It ends with a recommendation, such as "Your return is strong: consider increasing budget."</p>
<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Inconclusive tests are frustrating, so the tool should refuse to launch tests that are too small and suggest a bigger scope. And results showing low incrementality may reduce spend in the short term. I'd accept that, because advertisers who trust the numbers invest more over time.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Number of completed tests with a clear result, the share of advertisers who change budgets after a test, and their spend retention six months later.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Name the key concept (incrementality versus attribution).</li><li>Choose the advertiser segment.</li><li>Explain why the problem is hard.</li><li>Ideas, then a guided flow with example output.</li><li>Risks, then metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Defining the concept in one sentence shows mastery.</li><li>Example output with an uncertainty range makes it concrete.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Jargon without explanation, or dodging the business risk.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you handle advertisers too small to run a test?`,`Give them modeled estimates based on similar advertisers' test results, clearly labeled as estimates, and pool small advertisers into category-level studies.`],[`What if the test contradicts the attribution report?`,`That's expected. Attribution counts touches, incrementality counts cause. I'd explain the difference in the product and suggest using the test result to calibrate how much weight to put on attribution.`]]},

{n:10, q:`Design a privacy dashboard that helps people understand how their data is used in ads.`,
tests:`Clarity, trust, and balancing user control with a business that depends on data.`,
answer:`<p>The success of a privacy dashboard isn't how much it shows. It's whether a normal person leaves it understanding what's happening and feeling in control. Most privacy pages fail because they're complete but unreadable.</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> I'll focus on everyday users who aren't privacy experts, who feel vaguely uneasy, often after seeing an ad that felt too specific. That moment, "why am I seeing this?", is when they come looking.</p>
<p><strong>What they need.</strong> Three questions answered, in order: What do you know about me? Why did I see this particular ad? What can I change? Today these answers are usually scattered across several settings pages.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: a single summary page organized around those three questions. Two: a "why this ad" explanation attached to every ad, linking to the dashboard. Three: a simple privacy level: a slider from "more personalized" to "less personalized," which explains in plain words what each setting means.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> The summary page, with "why this ad" as the main entry point. At the top: "Here's what shapes your ads," with the main interest categories we've inferred, each with where it came from, like "because you searched for running shoes." Each one can be removed with a tap. Then a section showing which kinds of data are used: activity on Google, activity on partner sites, and so on, each with a toggle and a one-sentence explanation of what changes if you turn it off. At the bottom, one clear control to turn off personalized ads entirely.</p>
<p><strong>The trade-off.</strong> Clearer controls will lead some people to turn personalization off, which lowers ad revenue per user. I'd argue that's the right trade: trust is what keeps people using the products at all, and regulators increasingly expect this clarity. Also, people who can fix a wrong interest often get more relevant ads, not fewer.</p>
<p><strong>Design principles.</strong> Plain language at a reading level most people are comfortable with. No dark patterns: the "off" option has the same visual weight as the "on" option. And changes take effect quickly, with confirmation, so people can see that they worked.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> The main one is comprehension: in a quick survey after a visit, can people correctly answer "why did you see that ad?" I'd also track visits that end with an edit, and trust scores over time. The metric I'd watch but not optimize is the opt-out rate, because pushing it down would create the wrong incentives.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Define success (understanding and control).</li><li>User and trigger moment.</li><li>Three user questions, structured into a design.</li><li>Business trade-off, stated plainly.</li><li>Design principles, metrics, and a metric you deliberately don't optimize.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Identifying the entry point ("why this ad").</li><li>Choosing not to optimize opt-out rate shows integrity.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Treating privacy as a compliance checklist.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`Leadership is worried about revenue loss. What do you say?`,`I'd run a staged rollout and measure revenue impact directly. My hypothesis is that the opt-out rate is small and that removing wrong interests improves ad relevance. Either way, regulation is moving in this direction, so leading is better than being forced.`],[`How would you test comprehension?`,`Short in-product surveys after a visit, and moderated usability tests with people of different technical comfort, asking them to explain back what they saw.`]]},

{n:11, q:`How would you help new creators get discovered on YouTube Shorts?`,
tests:`Understanding of two-sided platforms, recommendation systems, and balancing creator growth with viewer quality.`,
answer:`<p>This is a two-sided problem. New creators need an audience, and viewers need their feed to stay good. If we push new creators without regard for quality, viewers leave, and then nobody gets discovered. So I'll keep both sides in mind.</p>
<p>Can I clarify what "new" means here? I'll assume creators in their first ninety days with under a thousand subscribers.</p>
<p><strong>Why it's hard for them.</strong> Recommendation systems favor content with a track record. A new creator's first videos get a small test audience, and if that audience is a poor match, the video looks bad and gets no further reach. Many creators quit after a few videos with a few dozen views. So the real problem isn't lack of exposure, it's getting a fair first test with the right viewers.</p>
<p><strong>Why it matters for YouTube.</strong> Creator supply is the engine. Every creator who quits is lost content, and the platform relies on a constant flow of new voices to stay fresh.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: a better "cold start" test, showing each new video to a small audience matched on topic, rather than a general audience. Two: a dedicated "new voices" slot, reserving a small share of the feed for promising new creators. Three: feedback for creators, showing them how their videos performed in that first test, like where viewers swiped away, so they can improve.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> I'd start with the matched cold start, combined with creator feedback. The matched test gives every video a fair first chance: shown to a few hundred people who watch similar content. If they respond well, the video earns wider reach. And the creator gets simple feedback: "Most viewers swiped away in the first two seconds. Try a stronger opening." That turns a failed video into learning, which keeps creators going.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-offs.</strong> Every slot given to an unproven creator is a slot not given to a proven one, so there's a short-term cost to viewer engagement. I'd cap the exploration share, measure the cost directly, and treat it as an investment in supply.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> For creators: the share of new creators who post a fifth video, and the share whose videos reach a thousand views in the first month. For viewers, as a guardrail: watch time and satisfaction for sessions that included new-creator content, compared with sessions that didn't.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Frame as two-sided and clarify "new."</li><li>Explain the root cause (cold start).</li><li>Why it matters to the business.</li><li>Ideas, pick a combination.</li><li>The exploration trade-off, then metrics for both sides.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Reframing from exposure to a fair first test.</li><li>Viewer guardrails prevent a one-sided answer.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>"Just boost new creators" without addressing quality.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you prevent people gaming the new-creator boost?`,`Tie eligibility to account age and authenticity signals, limit the boost per creator, and only extend reach based on real viewer responses, not the boost itself.`],[`What if new creators' content is lower quality on average?`,`That's expected. The goal is to find the good ones faster. The matched test is a cheap filter: most new videos get a small test and stop there.`]]},

{n:12, q:`Design a bicycle helmet for urban commuters.`,
tests:`Physical product thinking, understanding why people don't use a product, and designing for behavior, not just features.`,
answer:`<p>I'd start with the most important fact about helmets: the biggest problem isn't that they're poorly designed for crashes. It's that many commuters don't wear one at all. So I'll design for the reasons people don't wear them.</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> Adults who cycle to work in cities, including people using bike-share. I'll focus especially on occasional riders and bike-share users, because they're the least likely to have a helmet with them.</p>
<p><strong>Why people don't wear helmets.</strong> They're bulky to carry once you arrive: you walk into a meeting holding one. They cause "helmet hair" and sweat, which matters when you're going to work. They're easy to forget, especially for spontaneous bike-share trips. And some people just find them unattractive.</p>
<p>I'd prioritize portability, because it drives the "forgot it" problem and the "carrying it around" problem at the same time.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: a foldable or collapsible helmet that fits in a laptop bag. Two: a helmet with better ventilation and a removable, washable liner, to address sweat. Three: helmets offered at bike-share docks, which is more of a service than a product, and has hygiene challenges.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> A foldable helmet that collapses to about the size of a thick book and fits in a work bag. It meets the standard safety certification, which is non-negotiable. It has good ventilation and a removable liner. Design-wise, it should look like something a professional would wear, in neutral colors with a clean shape. I'd also include a small rear light built into the back, because visibility is the other big safety need for commuters.</p>
<p><strong>Risks and trade-offs.</strong> Folding mechanisms can compromise protection or feel flimsy, so certification testing is the first gate before anything else. Price will be higher than a basic helmet, so I'd position it as a daily-commute product worth paying for, like good headphones.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> For the business: sales and return rate. For the actual goal, I'd survey buyers on how often they wear it compared with their previous helmet. If wear rate goes up, we've solved the real problem.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Reframe: the problem is non-use.</li><li>Define the user and why they don't use it.</li><li>Prioritize the root reason.</li><li>Ideas, a specific product with a safety gate.</li><li>Risks, then a metric tied to the real goal.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>The reframe is the insight: the safest helmet is the one people wear.</li><li>Wear rate as the success metric, not just sales.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Loading it with tech features that don't address non-use.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you work with bike-share companies?`,`Offer a discounted helmet to their members, or partner on docks that sell or vend them. Their incentive is fewer injuries and better safety perception.`],[`Would you add smart features like crash detection?`,`Maybe later as a premium version. For version one, it adds cost and charging, which works against the goal of always having it with you.`]]},

{n:13, q:`Design a product that helps drivers find parking.`,
tests:`Understanding of real-time data problems, marketplace dynamics, and choosing a feasible starting point.`,
answer:`<p>Finding parking is really a data problem: the information about which spots are free changes minute by minute, and nobody has it reliably. So I'll focus on where we can get trustworthy data. Is it fine to assume I'm designing within Google Maps?</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> Drivers heading into busy urban areas, especially to places they don't go often, like a restaurant or a doctor's appointment. Regular commuters usually already have a routine or a monthly spot.</p>
<p><strong>What's hard.</strong> They don't know whether parking will be easy or hard until they arrive. They circle blocks looking for a spot, which wastes time and adds traffic. They don't know prices or rules, like time limits or permit zones. And they're often late because they didn't plan for parking time.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: a parking difficulty forecast for the destination, based on patterns, like "Parking is usually hard here on Friday evenings." Two: navigation straight to a garage with real-time availability and prices, using data from garage operators. Three: street spot detection using data from cars or phones, predicting where spots are likely free.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> A combination of the first two, because both use data we can actually get. When you set a destination, Maps shows a parking difficulty indicator and, if it's hard, suggests leaving a few minutes earlier. It then offers "navigate to parking," suggesting a nearby garage with availability, price, and walking time to your destination. Where the operator partners with us, you can even reserve and pay in Maps.</p>
<p>I'd deliberately not start with predicting free street spots. It's the most exciting idea, but the data is noisy, and sending people to a spot that's gone is worse than no answer.</p>
<p><strong>Risks.</strong> Garage data quality varies, so I'd start in cities with good partners. And the forecast can be wrong on unusual days like events, so I'd use event calendars too.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Time from arriving near the destination to parking, which we can estimate from location patterns. Usage of "navigate to parking," reservations completed, and, for trust, how often people who accept a suggestion actually park there.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Frame as a data problem and clarify context.</li><li>Choose the user who struggles most.</li><li>Pain points and three ideas at different feasibility levels.</li><li>Pick what's feasible, and say explicitly what you're not doing.</li><li>Risks, metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Explicitly deprioritizing the flashy idea for a reason.</li><li>Accounting for data quality as a product risk.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Assuming perfect real-time data exists.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you get garages to share data?`,`Offer them demand: drivers routed to them and reservations sold. Start with large operators who already have digital systems.`],[`How would you monetize?`,`A booking fee on reservations, paid by the garage or the driver, and potentially promoted placement for garages, clearly labeled.`]]},

{n:14, q:`How would you improve Google Calendar for teams spread across many time zones?`,
tests:`Understanding collaboration pain, fairness, and a feature that fits naturally into an existing product.`,
answer:`<p>Let me first check the kind of team. I'll assume a company team of eight to fifteen people across, say, the US, Europe, and India, meeting several times a week.</p>
<p><strong>What's hard for them.</strong> Three things. First, finding a time: the overlap in working hours is small or zero, so scheduling takes a lot of back-and-forth. Second, fairness: the same people, often those far from headquarters, keep taking the early morning or late-night calls. Over time, that's a real morale issue. Third, mistakes: people get confused about time zones and daylight saving changes, and someone shows up an hour early or late.</p>
<p>I'd prioritize fairness. The scheduling problem gets partly solved by existing features like suggested times, but nobody is tracking fairness, and it quietly affects retention.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: when scheduling, show each attendee's local time with color coding for normal hours, early, late, and very late. Two: a "rotate" option for recurring meetings that alternates the time so the inconvenient slot is shared. Three: a team-level report showing how many out-of-hours meetings each person had this month.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> I'd combine the first two. When you create a meeting, you see a row for each person with their local time, colored green, yellow, or red. For recurring meetings, you can choose "rotate across time zones," and Calendar alternates between two or three times so the pain is shared. The organizer also gets a gentle note like "Priya has had four late meetings this week."</p>
<p><strong>Trade-offs.</strong> Rotating times can confuse people, so the event needs to clearly show "This week's time" and send reminders when the time shifts. The fairness note must be private to the organizer, so it doesn't feel like public shaming.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> The share of recurring cross-time-zone meetings using rotation, the number of out-of-hours meetings per person, and how evenly those are spread across a team. Also, fewer reschedules and fewer late joins.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Clarify the team shape.</li><li>Three pain points, prioritize the neglected one.</li><li>Ideas, combine into one flow.</li><li>Trade-offs and sensitivity.</li><li>Metrics, including one for fairness.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Picking fairness, which most candidates miss.</li><li>A metric for how evenly pain is spread, not just the total.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Proposing AI scheduling assistants without a specific problem.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you measure fairness?`,`For each team, the spread of out-of-hours meetings across members, for example the ratio between the person with the most and the average. Lower is fairer.`],[`Would you let people set protected hours?`,`Yes. Calendar already supports working hours. I'd let people set hard limits, and scheduling suggestions would respect them.`]]},

{n:15, q:`Design a voice assistant feature for people over 70.`,
tests:`Designing for a population often poorly served by tech, with empathy and respect, not condescension.`,
answer:`<p>Let me avoid designing for a stereotype. People over seventy vary a lot, from very tech-savvy to not at all. I'll focus on those who live independently, own a smart speaker, often as a gift from family, but only use it for music or the weather.</p>
<p><strong>What would help them.</strong> The biggest opportunity I see is managing daily routines, particularly medications. Many older adults take several medicines at different times. Missing a dose or double-dosing is common and can be serious. Current reminders on phones are easy to miss or dismiss.</p>
<p>Other needs include staying in touch with family, and help in an emergency, but medication has the clearest, most frequent value, and it's a natural fit for voice, because you don't need to find a screen.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: medication reminders by voice that ask for confirmation: "It's 8 a.m., time for your blood pressure pill. Did you take it?" Two: a simple way for a family member to set up the schedule remotely. Three: if a dose is confirmed as missed, or there's no answer, a gentle follow-up, and optionally, a notification to a family member.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> All three together, as one feature. The family member, or the person themselves, sets up the medication schedule in an app. At each time, the speaker says the reminder, in plain words and at a comfortable pace. The person answers "yes" or "not yet." If "not yet," it reminds again in fifteen minutes. If no response in an hour, it can send a message to a chosen contact, with the person's consent. The person can also ask, "Did I take my morning pills?" which helps with the common worry of not remembering.</p>
<p><strong>Design choices.</strong> Slower speech and a clear voice. Forgiving recognition: any reasonable phrasing counts as yes. Never talking down to the person. And the older adult controls whether family gets notified.</p>
<p><strong>Risks.</strong> This is close to health, so we must be careful not to give medical advice. It's a reminder tool, not a doctor. If the speaker is offline, reminders fail, so we'd need a fallback, like a phone alert, and a daily self-check.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> The share of scheduled doses confirmed, the number of people still using it after three months, and qualitative feedback from both the person and family about peace of mind.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Avoid stereotypes and choose a specific segment.</li><li>Find the highest-frequency, highest-value need.</li><li>Combine ideas into one coherent feature.</li><li>Design choices for this audience.</li><li>Health risks and reliability, then metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>"Did I take my pills?" addresses a real, subtle need.</li><li>Consent and control remain with the older adult.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Condescending design choices, like "make everything big and simple."</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you handle privacy with health data?`,`Store the schedule securely, don't use it for advertising, and make it clear who can see what. Ideally, the person sees and approves any family access.`],[`How would you distribute this?`,`Through family members as buyers, and potentially through pharmacies or health insurers, who benefit from better medication adherence.`]]},

{n:16, q:`Redesign Google Translate for travelers with poor connectivity.`,
tests:`Designing for constraints, reliability over features, and making technical trade-offs understandable.`,
answer:`<p>The key constraint here is that the product must work when the network doesn't. That changes the priorities: reliability and speed matter more than accuracy on rare phrases.</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> Travelers in a country where they don't speak the language, in places with weak or expensive data, like rural areas, subways, or abroad without a local SIM.</p>
<p><strong>What they need, in order.</strong> Getting through essential moments: asking for directions, ordering food, reading signs and menus, and handling an emergency. Beyond that, having conversations with locals is a nice-to-have.</p>
<p><strong>What goes wrong today.</strong> Offline language packs exist, but many travelers don't know to download them before they leave. When they need translation, they're already offline. And offline quality is lower, especially for photo and conversation modes.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: prompt the download at the right moment, for example when a flight booking or trip appears in Gmail or Maps, or when the phone detects roaming. Two: a "travel kit" pack that includes not just the language model, but common phrases for eating, transport, and emergencies, with audio. Three: seamless fallback: use the online model when there's signal and quietly switch to offline when there isn't, without error screens.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> Prompting the download at the right moment is the highest-leverage fix, because offline mode already exists, but people don't have it. So: "You're going to Japan on Friday. Download Japanese for offline use? It's 60 MB, best on Wi-Fi." Paired with the travel kit, so there's a curated, fast phrasebook for high-stakes moments like "I'm allergic to peanuts."</p>
<p>And when offline, I'd make the app honest: a small "offline" label and a note that complex sentences may be less accurate, suggesting simple phrasing.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-offs.</strong> Storage: language packs take space, so we'd offer a smaller basic pack. And prompting at the right time uses signals like email bookings, so it has to follow privacy settings and be easy to turn off.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> The share of international travelers who have an offline pack before they need it, the success rate of offline translations measured by users not retrying, and use of the phrasebook in emergency categories.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Name the constraint and how it changes priorities.</li><li>User and prioritized needs.</li><li>Find the real failure (not downloaded ahead).</li><li>Highest-leverage fix, plus supporting features.</li><li>Trade-offs, metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Recognizing that the capability exists but isn't used. It's a timing problem.</li><li>Honest UI when quality drops.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Proposing a totally new offline model as the first step.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What if someone didn't download a pack and has no signal?`,`A tiny built-in emergency phrasebook for the most common languages that ships with the app, covering help, hospital, police, and allergies.`],[`How would you improve offline quality?`,`Smaller, specialized on-device models for the travel domain. They can be much better at menus and directions than a general model of the same size.`]]},

{n:17, q:`Design a mobile app for a public library.`,
tests:`Understanding the real value of an institution beyond its obvious service, and designing for a broad population.`,
answer:`<p>When I think about a library app, the easy answer is "search the catalog and reserve books." Most library apps already do that, and people still don't use them much. So I'll ask what the library really offers and who's missing out.</p>
<p><strong>What a library offers.</strong> Books, yes, but also e-books and audiobooks, free classes, events for kids, computers and internet, meeting rooms, and help from librarians. Many residents don't know most of that exists.</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> I'll focus on parents of young children. They're heavy potential users: they need a constant supply of new books, they value free events, and they have little time. Many only visit occasionally because it's hard to keep up with what's happening and when books are due.</p>
<p><strong>What's hard.</strong> Keeping track of due dates across many kids' books and avoiding fines. Finding out about story times and events in time. And choosing books quickly, for example what's good for a four-year-old who loves dinosaurs.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: a family account that tracks everyone's loans in one place, with automatic renewals where possible. Two: a personalized weekly "what's on" based on kids' ages. Three: a reading list builder: pick ages and interests, get a list of available books, and have them placed on hold for pickup.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> A family home screen. At the top: what's due, with one-tap renewal, and a clear "nothing is overdue" state. Then: "This week for your family," showing story times and events matching the kids' ages, with a reminder button. And a "Ready for pickup" section, powered by a simple request: "Five picture books about dinosaurs for age four," and librarians or the system put them aside.</p>
<p><strong>Considerations.</strong> Libraries serve everyone, including people without smartphones, so this can't replace in-person services. It needs to be accessible and multilingual. And libraries have small budgets, so the solution needs to work with their existing systems.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Active family accounts, loans per family per month, event attendance from app reminders, and fewer overdue items.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Reject the obvious answer and ask what the institution really offers.</li><li>Pick an underserved, high-value segment.</li><li>Pain points, ideas, one home screen.</li><li>Equity and budget considerations.</li><li>Metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Thinking about the library's full offering, not just the catalog.</li><li>Respecting the constraints of a public institution.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Designing a Goodreads clone.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you drive adoption?`,`Sign families up at story time and at the desk, with a librarian helping them set it up in a minute. Put a QR code on every receipt.`],[`How would you measure the library's broader impact?`,`Program attendance, new cardholders, and the share of residents who used any library service in the past year, which is how libraries often justify their budgets.`]]},

{n:18, q:`How would you improve Gmail search?`,
tests:`Understanding search as a product, identifying specific failure modes, and weighing complexity against the user's mental model.`,
answer:`<p>To improve search, I'd first want to know where it fails. Let me think about what people search for in email and why they don't find it.</p>
<p><strong>What people search for.</strong> Mostly, they're looking for something specific they know exists: a flight confirmation, a document someone sent, a message from a person about a topic. It's usually retrieval, not discovery.</p>
<p><strong>Where search fails.</strong> First, fuzzy memory: "that PDF Sarah sent a few months ago about the budget." People remember the person, the rough time, and the type of item, not the words. Second, attachments: searching for text inside a PDF or a picture of a receipt doesn't always work. Third, the result list: when many emails match, you scroll through dozens of threads to find the one with the attachment.</p>
<p>I'd prioritize the fuzzy memory problem, because it's the most common and it's where people give up.</p>
<p><strong>Ideas.</strong> One: natural-language queries, like "PDF from Sarah about budget last spring," translated into filters behind the scenes. Two: direct answers: for "my flight to Chicago," show the flight card with the confirmation number at the top, not just a list. Three: results that group by type, so attachments, links, and events appear as their own sections.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> Natural-language search with transparent filters. When you type "PDF from Sarah about budget last spring," the search bar shows the chips it understood: "From: Sarah," "Has: PDF," "Date: March to May," "Words: budget." Each chip can be removed or edited. That keeps it understandable: people can see and fix what the system did, rather than wonder why a result is missing. For common types, like flights and packages, I'd show a direct answer card at the top.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-offs.</strong> Natural-language understanding can misinterpret queries, so the chips are both the explanation and the correction mechanism. And it has to be fast; search in email must feel instant.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> The share of searches where the user opens a result without reformulating the query, time to open a result, and how often people edit the chips, which tells us where the interpretation is wrong.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Understand the task (retrieval, not discovery).</li><li>Specific failure modes, prioritize one.</li><li>Ideas and a solution with a transparency mechanism.</li><li>Trade-offs, metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>"Fuzzy memory" is a crisp, relatable insight.</li><li>Editable chips solve the trust problem of natural-language search.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>"Add AI to search" without explaining the failure mode.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you evaluate search quality offline?`,`Build a test set of real queries (with consent or using internal volunteers) with the right answer labeled, and track whether the right email appears in the top results.`],[`What about privacy?`,`Processing the user's own mailbox for their own search is expected, but I'd ensure models don't mix data across users, and explain it clearly.`]]},

{n:19, q:`Design a debugging experience for someone whose website tags aren't firing.`,
tests:`Domain knowledge of tagging, empathy for a non-technical user, and turning an expert task into guided steps.`,
answer:`<p>First, let me check who's debugging. A developer and a marketer have very different needs. I'll assume a marketer at a small or mid-sized business who set up tags in a tag manager, sees conversions at zero, and doesn't have a developer available.</p>
<p><strong>What they're feeling.</strong> Anxiety. They're spending money on ads and the reporting shows nothing. They don't know if the ads are failing or the tracking is broken. The worst outcome for them is not knowing where to start.</p>
<p><strong>Why tags fail.</strong> The common causes are: the container isn't installed on the page, or not on every page; the trigger condition doesn't match, for example a thank-you page URL changed; a consent banner is blocking tags until the user accepts; the tag was changed but never published; or there's a naming mismatch between the tag and the conversion action. Most of these are checkable automatically.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> A guided "Why isn't my tag working?" checker. The user picks the tag, enters their website address, and it runs through checks, showing results as a checklist:</p>
<ul><li>"Tag manager found on your homepage: yes."</li><li>"Tag manager found on your thank-you page: no. This is likely the problem."</li><li>"Latest changes published: yes."</li><li>"Consent banner detected: tags wait for consent. This is normal, but expect fewer conversions."</li></ul>
<p>For each failure, it gives one clear next step in plain language, and where possible a fix button, like "Publish your changes." If it needs a developer, it generates a short message the marketer can forward, with exactly what to add and where.</p>
<p>For deeper cases, there's an option to launch a live preview, where the user browses their site and sees each tag light up or not, with the reason.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-offs.</strong> Automated checks can't see everything, like pages behind a login. So the tool should say when it couldn't check something, rather than giving false confidence.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> The share of debug sessions that end with a tag firing within twenty-four hours, reduction in support tickets about tracking, and time from first failed conversion to a fix.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Clarify the user (marketer versus developer).</li><li>Their emotional state and main fear.</li><li>Root causes from domain knowledge.</li><li>A guided checklist with plain-language fixes.</li><li>Limits, metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Listing real failure causes shows you know the product.</li><li>The "message for your developer" is a thoughtful handoff.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Designing only for developers, or only a documentation page.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you prioritize which checks to build first?`,`By frequency in support tickets. I'd guess container missing, unpublished changes, and trigger mismatch cover most cases.`],[`How would you proactively catch these problems?`,`Monitor conversions per tag and alert when they drop to zero or fall sharply, linking directly to the checker.`]]},

{n:20, q:`Design onboarding for an agency managing first-party data for 50 advertiser accounts.`,
tests:`B2B product thinking: designing for scale, repeatable workflows, permissions, and compliance.`,
answer:`<p>This is a very different user from a single advertiser. The agency's problem is doing the same setup fifty times, correctly, with each client's data kept separate and properly consented. So I'll design for scale and control.</p>
<p><strong>Who.</strong> The agency's data or operations lead, who's responsible for connecting each client's first-party data, like customer lists or CRM conversions, to their ad accounts. They report to account managers who own client relationships.</p>
<p><strong>What's hard.</strong> Repetition: each account needs the same setup steps, and doing them one by one takes weeks. Consistency: small differences across accounts cause errors that are hard to find later. Permissions and consent: each client must authorize data use, and the agency must prove it. And monitoring: once set up, they need to know when any of the fifty connections breaks.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd build.</strong> Onboarding in three parts.</p>
<p>First, templates. The agency sets up one configuration as a template, for example: upload customer lists weekly, map these fields, use these conversion actions. Then they apply it to many accounts at once, adjusting only what differs, like the data source.</p>
<p>Second, a client consent flow. For each client, the agency sends a request, and the client approves access and confirms that their customer data was collected with appropriate consent. This creates a record the agency can show later.</p>
<p>Third, a status dashboard. All fifty accounts in one table: connected, waiting for client approval, error, or healthy. Errors show the cause and the fix. Alerts go to the right person when an account's data stops flowing.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-offs.</strong> Bulk actions are powerful and dangerous: one mistake applies to fifty clients. So bulk changes show a preview of exactly what will change, and can be rolled back. Data must stay separated by client, with no chance of one client's data appearing in another's account.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Time to onboard all accounts, the share of accounts healthy after thirty days, errors per account, and agency retention and expansion to more clients.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Contrast with a single advertiser to show understanding.</li><li>The user and their role.</li><li>Pain points: repetition, consistency, consent, monitoring.</li><li>A three-part solution.</li><li>Safety for bulk actions and data separation.</li><li>Metrics.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Designing for the agency's scale problem, not the individual setup.</li><li>Consent records as a product feature.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Designing single-account onboarding and saying "repeat fifty times."</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you handle a client who leaves the agency?`,`A clean offboarding flow: revoke the agency's access, keep the client's data and configuration in their own account, and log the change.`],[`What if clients use different CRM systems?`,`Templates would have a data-source step that supports the main connectors, with field mapping saved per source type, so common CRMs get reusable mappings.`]]}
]);
